.TH SIMPLE_SHELL 1 "August 2026" "hsh 1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
hsh \- a simple UNIX command line interpreter
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ./hsh
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B hsh
is a small shell that reads command lines and runs them. It works in two
modes. In interactive mode it prints the prompt
.B ($)
and waits for you to type a command. In non\-interactive mode it reads
commands from a pipe or a file, one command per line, with no prompt.
.PP
Each line is split into words separated by spaces or tabs. The first word
is the program to run and the remaining words are passed to it as
arguments. The command must be given as a full path, for example
.B /bin/ls
\- this version does not search the
.B PATH
environment variable.
.PP
After a command finishes, the prompt is shown again (in interactive mode)
and the shell waits for the next line.
.SH EXECUTION
For every command,
.B hsh
creates a child process with
.BR fork (2)
and replaces it with the requested program using
.BR execve (2).
The shell (the parent) waits for the child to finish with
.BR waitpid (2)
before reading the next command.
.SH EXIT
The shell exits when it reaches end\-of\-file, with the exit status of the
last command it ran. In interactive mode end\-of\-file is sent by pressing
.B Ctrl+D
on an empty line.
.SH ERRORS
If a command cannot be run, an error message is printed to standard error
and the shell continues. It matches the message
.BR sh (1)
prints, except the program name is
.BR argv[0]
instead of
.BR sh :
.PP
.RS
./hsh: 1: qwerty: not found
.RE
.PP
The number is the line number of the command that failed.
.SH EXAMPLES
Interactive:
.PP
.RS
$ ./hsh
.br
($) /bin/ls
.br
($) /bin/echo hello world
.br
($) Ctrl+D
.RE
.PP
Non\-interactive:
.PP
.RS
$ echo "/bin/ls" | ./hsh
.RE
.SH BUGS
No PATH search, no built\-in commands, and no handling of pipes,
redirections, logical operators, or quoting.
